---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CopyCommand from '../components/copyCommand';
import { githubUrl, skillInstallCommand, skillUseCommand } from '../lib/nav';
import { withBase } from '../lib/base';

const skillUrl = `${githubUrl}/blob/HEAD/skills/lattice/SKILL.md`;

const contents = [
	{
		file: 'SKILL.md',
		blurb:
			'How to orient in a repo that uses Lattice, run and preview tasks, declare a workspace, and decide what to cache.',
	},
	{
		file: 'references/configuration.md',
		blurb: 'Every lattice.json field, its type, its default, and the error it raises when it is wrong.',
	},
	{
		file: 'references/cli.md',
		blurb: 'Every command and flag, the exit codes, the environment variables, and the precedence between them.',
	},
	{
		file: 'references/toolchains.md',
		blurb: 'The built-in driver table, the engines that can be version-checked by name, and how provisioning works.',
	},
	{
		file: 'references/troubleshooting.md',
		blurb: 'Symptom to cause to fix, for the failures a run actually produces.',
	},
];
---

<Layout
	title="For agents · Lattice"
	description="Install the Lattice skill so your coding agent knows how to read a lattice.json, run tasks, and read a failure."
>
	<section class="px-3 pt-5 pb-4">
		<div class="mx-auto" style="max-width: 48rem;">
			<h1 class="fw-bold mb-3" style="font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 3.75rem); letter-spacing: -0.03em;">
				For agents
			</h1>
			<p class="fs-5 mb-0" style="color: var(--text-muted);">
				Lattice is new, so a coding agent has no working knowledge of it and will guess at
				<code>lattice.json</code> from the shape of other config files. This skill replaces the guess:
				the commands, the fields, the tables, and the failures, written for an agent to read.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="px-3 pb-5">
		<div class="mx-auto" style="max-width: 48rem;">
			<h2 class="h4 fw-bold mb-3">Install it</h2>
			<CopyCommand client:visible command={skillInstallCommand} />
			<p class="mt-3 mb-4" style="color: var(--text-subtle); line-height: 1.65;">
				Run it in the repo you want the skill in. The CLI finds the agents you have installed and writes
				the skill where each of them looks for it, then records what it installed in
				<code>skills-lock.json</code>. Add <code>--global</code> to install it once for every project
				instead, or <code>npx skills update lattice</code> later to pull a newer copy.
			</p>

			<h2 class="h4 fw-bold mb-3">Or load it for one session</h2>
			<CopyCommand client:visible command={skillUseCommand} />
			<p class="mt-3 mb-0" style="color: var(--text-subtle); line-height: 1.65;">
				This prints the skill as a prompt without writing anything to disk — useful for a one-off task or
				for an agent the installer doesn't know about.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="px-3 pb-5">
		<div class="mx-auto" style="max-width: 48rem;">
			<h2 class="h4 fw-bold mb-3">What it covers</h2>
			<p class="mb-4" style="color: var(--text-subtle); line-height: 1.65;">
				One instruction file plus four references the agent reads only when it needs them. Everything in
				them is drawn from the same source as these docs, so an agent working from the skill and a person
				working from the site get the same answer.
			</p>
			<div class="list-group list-group-flush">
				{
					contents.map((item) => (
						<div class="list-group-item px-0 py-3" style="background: transparent; border-color: var(--border-subtle);">
							<code class="d-block mb-1" style="color: var(--text); font-size: 0.85rem;">
								{item.file}
							</code>
							<span class="d-block" style="color: var(--text-subtle); line-height: 1.6;">
								{item.blurb}
							</span>
						</div>
					))
				}
			</div>
			<p class="mt-4 mb-0" style="color: var(--text-subtle); line-height: 1.65;">
				The source lives in the repo at
				<a href={skillUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>skills/lattice/</code></a>. Read it
				yourself before you install it — it is a few pages of plain Markdown, and it is the same file your
				agent will act on.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="px-3 pb-5">
		<div class="mx-auto" style="max-width: 48rem;">
			<h2 class="h4 fw-bold mb-3">Without the skill</h2>
			<p class="mb-0" style="color: var(--text-subtle); line-height: 1.65;">
				An agent that can read files and run commands can get most of the way on its own. Point it at
				<code>lattice.json</code>, then have it run
				<code>lattice run &lt;task&gt; --dry-run</code>, which validates the config and prints every
				resolved command without executing anything. <code>lattice --help</code> and each subcommand's
				<code>--help</code> are the authoritative flag list. The
				<a href={withBase('/docs/configuration')}>configuration</a> and
				<a href={withBase('/docs/cli')}>CLI</a> pages are the same reference material the skill carries,
				and every page on this site is Markdown in the repo.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</Layout>
